<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Philip the Bold</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1342–1404</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Philip II, Duke of Burgundy</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Philip the Bold was the youngest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-ii-of-france">John II of France</a>, who granted him the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> in 1363. His marriage in 1369 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margaret-iii-countess-of-flanders">Margaret of Flanders</a> brought him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>, Artois and the Free County of Burgundy on her father's death in 1384, founding the Burgundian State of the Valois dukes. He was also one of the leading regents for his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-vi-of-france">Charles VI of France</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philip-the-bold.jpg" alt="Philip the Bold" width="320" height="416" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: anonymous · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:16th-century_unknown_painters_-_Philip_the_Bold_-_WGA23677.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
